{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was Count of Tripoli from approximately 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Amalric I of Jerusalem and Agnes of Courtenay. Raymond III was a member of the House of Anjou and played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century.\n\n* **Reign and Military Campaigns**\nRaymond III became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after the death of his father Amalric I. However the count of Tripoli at the time was not recognized by the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I. In 1152 Raymond III became the Count of Tripoli through the support of the King of Jerusalem. Raymond III ruled Tripoli for nearly three decades. In 1163 Raymond III participated in the Second Crusade. He led a campaign in Syria and fought against the Ayyubid dynasty led by Saladin. Raymond III was involved in various battles and skirmishes throughout his reign and was known for his military prowess.\n\n* **Marriage and Family**\nRaymond III married Eschive of Toulouse and had several children with her. One of their sons Raymond IV became the Count of Tripoli after his father's death in 1187. Raymond III also had a daughter Marie who married the Prince of Antioch Bohemond III. Raymond III's family played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century.\n\n* **Death and Legacy**\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin. The Battle of Hattin was a decisive victory for the Ayyubid dynasty led by Saladin. The battle led to the loss of Jerusalem and the fall of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond III's legacy is complex and multifaceted. He played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century and was known for his military prowess. However his death in 1187 marked the beginning of the end of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the rise of the Ayyubid dynasty.", "logprobs